## Solution

We evaluate

$$I = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} e^{-e^{x}-e^{-x}+x}\,dx.$$

**Step 1: Substitution.** Let $t = e^{x}$, so $dt = e^{x}\,dx = t\,dx$, i.e. $dx = dt/t$. As $x$ runs from $-\infty$ to $+\infty$, $t$ runs from $0$ to $+\infty$. The exponent becomes

$$-e^{x}-e^{-x}+x = -t - \frac{1}{t} + \ln t,$$

hence

$$e^{-e^{x}-e^{-x}+x} = e^{-t-1/t+\ln t} = t\,e^{-t-1/t}.$$

Therefore

$$I = \int_{0}^{\infty} t\,e^{-t-1/t}\,\frac{dt}{t} = \int_{0}^{\infty} e^{-t-1/t}\,dt. \tag{1}$$

**Step 2: Identify the Bessel representation.** The modified Bessel function of the second kind has the integral representation (valid for $\operatorname{Re} z > 0$):

$$K_{\nu}(z) = \frac{1}{2}\Bigl(\frac{z}{2}\Bigr)^{\!\nu}\int_{0}^{\infty} t^{-\nu-1}\,e^{-t - z^{2}/(4t)}\,dt. \tag{2}$$

Set $z = 2$ (so $z^{2}/4 = 1$) and $\nu = -1$ (so $t^{-\nu-1} = t^{0} = 1$ and $(z/2)^{\nu} = 1^{-1} = 1$):

$$K_{-1}(2) = \frac{1}{2}\int_{0}^{\infty} e^{-t-1/t}\,dt. \tag{3}$$

Using the symmetry $K_{-\nu}(z) = K_{\nu}(z)$, we have $K_{-1}(2) = K_{1}(2)$.

**Step 3: Conclude.** Combining (1) and (3),

$$I = \int_{0}^{\infty} e^{-t-1/t}\,dt = 2\,K_{-1}(2) = 2\,K_{1}(2).$$

**Numerical check.** $K_{1}(2) \approx 0.1398658818$, so $I \approx 0.2797317636$. A direct numerical evaluation of the original integral (the integrand peaks near $x = \ln\varphi \approx 0.481$ with peak value $\approx 0.173$, over a width of order $1$) is consistent with this value.

**Answer.** Yes, a closed form exists:

$$\boxed{\,I = 2\,K_{1}(2)\,}$$

where $K_{1}$ is the modified Bessel function of the second kind of order $1$. This does not reduce to elementary functions, but it is a standard named special-function closed form.

